WEBVTT

00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:03.921
Good news, everyone.

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:08.675
Tomorrow, you'll be making a delivery
to Ebola 9, the virus planet.

00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:10.052
Why can't they go today?

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:14.515
Because tonight's a special night
and I want all of you to be alive.

00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:17.643
It's the Academy of Inventors'
annual symposium.

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:19.645
Wow. I love symposia.

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:22.356
It's the event of the scientific season.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:27.528
Every member presents an invention
and the best one wins the Academy prize.

00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:28.654
Sounds boring.

00:00:28.737 --> 00:00:31.281
Oh, my, yes, but not this year

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:34.284
because my latest invention is unbeatable.

00:00:34.368 --> 00:00:35.410
Behold,

00:00:35.494 --> 00:00:37.204
the death clock.

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:39.373
Simply jam your finger in the hole

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:43.335
and this readout tells you
exactly how long you have left to live.

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:44.670
Does it really work?

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:47.631
Well, it's occasionally off
by a few seconds,

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.508
what with free will and all.

00:00:49.591 --> 00:00:50.592
Sounds like fun.

00:00:50.676 --> 00:00:52.135
How long do I have to live?

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.224
Ooh, dibs on his CD player!

00:01:37.389 --> 00:01:38.849
Who's the gross nerd?

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.435
That's me, at the very first symposium.

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:44.271
I'm the Academy's
oldest living member, you know.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.481
These youngsters all look up to me.

00:01:46.565 --> 00:01:48.108
Well, well, well.

00:01:48.192 --> 00:01:51.028
Look who decided
to show his wrinkled face.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.739
Why don't you
just leave me alone, Wernstrom?

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:56.283
Face it, Farnsworth, you're over the hill.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:59.119
It's time to leave science
to the 120-year-olds.

00:01:59.203 --> 00:02:01.830
You young turks think you know everything.

00:02:01.914 --> 00:02:04.917
I was inventing things
when you were barely turning senile.

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:09.505
Go home before
you embarrass yourself, old man.

00:02:09.588 --> 00:02:10.839
Now, if you'll excuse me,

00:02:10.923 --> 00:02:13.217
I'm going to take a nap
before the ceremonies.

00:02:14.468 --> 00:02:15.677
Who's that jerk?

00:02:15.761 --> 00:02:17.095
A hundred years ago,

00:02:17.179 --> 00:02:21.016
he was my most promising student
at Mars University,

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:24.144
but then, after one fateful pop quiz...

00:02:26.063 --> 00:02:27.105
A-minus?

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:29.816
No one gives Ogden Wernstrom an A-minus.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.528
I'm sorry, but penmanship counts.

00:02:32.611 --> 00:02:36.448
I swear I'll have my revenge
if it takes me a hundred years.

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:39.159
And here it is

00:02:39.243 --> 00:02:42.955
Slightly over 99 years later
and still no revenge.

00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:44.873
I'm essentially in the clear.

00:02:48.669 --> 00:02:51.672
I've been perusing
your fortified wine list

00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:55.008
and I've selected the '71 Hobo's Delight,

00:02:55.092 --> 00:03:00.097
the '57 Chateau Part-ay
and the '66 Thunder-Schewitz.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.641
Exquisite choices, sir.

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:06.520
And mix them all together in a big jug.

00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:09.398
Welcome to this year's Academy symposium.

00:03:09.481 --> 00:03:11.149
I'm your host, Ron Popeil,

00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:14.570
inventor of Mr. Microphone,
the spray-on toupee

00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:18.448
and, of course, the technology
to keep human heads alive in jars.

00:03:22.052 --> 00:03:23.595
But wait, there's more!

00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:26.098
We've got a whole lineup
of inventors tonight,

00:03:26.181 --> 00:03:28.517
starting with
that up-and-coming young star,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.977
Ogden Wernstrom.

00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:32.312
Boo!

00:03:32.396 --> 00:03:33.563
More wine!

00:03:33.647 --> 00:03:35.732
Distinguished members of the Academy,

00:03:35.816 --> 00:03:38.568
I present to you the reverse scuba suit.

00:03:38.652 --> 00:03:39.653
Observe.

00:03:39.987 --> 00:03:40.988
Fetch.

00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:55.002
Now sit.

00:03:55.877 --> 00:03:57.045
I said sit!

00:03:57.129 --> 00:03:58.213
Bad fish!

00:04:01.925 --> 00:04:04.052
- Oh...
- Don't worry, Professor.

00:04:04.136 --> 00:04:06.596
It's no competition for your death clock.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.641
And what will you be presenting
this evening, Grandpa?

00:04:09.725 --> 00:04:13.729
Let's just say it'll put
you young whippersnappers in your place.

00:04:13.812 --> 00:04:15.105
I just hope it's not as lame

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.357
as that death clock
you presented last year.

00:04:17.441 --> 00:04:19.985
- Eh, "last year," you say?
- That's right.

00:04:20.068 --> 00:04:21.320
Oh, my.

00:04:21.403 --> 00:04:24.197
Did it put you young whippersnappers
in your place?

00:04:24.281 --> 00:04:25.282
Hardly.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.284
We laughed until our teeth fell out.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.785
Come along, Cinnamon.

00:04:30.329 --> 00:04:31.371
Oh, dear.

00:04:31.455 --> 00:04:34.499
I'll have to invent something new
in the next 10 minutes,

00:04:34.583 --> 00:04:37.210
perhaps some sort of death clock.

00:04:37.714 --> 00:04:39.049
Uh, professor?

00:04:48.889 --> 00:04:51.767
Our last presentation
comes from our oldest member,

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.435
Professor Hubert Farnsworth.

00:04:54.019 --> 00:04:55.103
Professor?

00:04:55.187 --> 00:04:58.065
Uh... Just a second. Just a second.

00:04:58.148 --> 00:05:00.067
Pencils down, prune face.

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:05.364
Oh, yes, here I am.
Okay, now. Hello there.

00:05:05.447 --> 00:05:09.952
Now, we all know telescopes
allow us to see distant objects,

00:05:10.035 --> 00:05:13.246
but what if we want
to smell distant objects?

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.082
Well, now we can,

00:05:15.165 --> 00:05:17.542
thanks to my new invention,

00:05:17.626 --> 00:05:19.336
the Smelloscope.

00:05:21.838 --> 00:05:25.175
The odor travels past
this coffee stain here,

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:29.179
around the olive pit
and into this cigar burn.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:31.890
And this appears to be a... a...

00:05:31.974 --> 00:05:34.268
doodle of myself as a cowboy.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.147
But the Smelloscope
is brilliant, I tell you!

00:05:39.231 --> 00:05:42.442
Think of the astronomical odors
you'll smell, thanks to me!

00:05:47.072 --> 00:05:48.073
Oh, my.

00:05:52.411 --> 00:05:55.247
I've waited a hundred years
for this, Farnsworth.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.792
I give your invention
the worst grade imaginable,

00:05:58.875 --> 00:06:02.129
an A-minus-minus!

00:06:06.992 --> 00:06:09.203
And now for the presentation of the award.

00:06:09.286 --> 00:06:12.456
Listen, folks,
I'm practically giving this prize away

00:06:12.539 --> 00:06:15.501
to Dr. Wernstrom for his fish thingy.

00:06:26.178 --> 00:06:31.266
Perhaps 149 is just too old
to be a scientist.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.061
- Yep.
- No, Professor. Don't give up.

00:06:34.144 --> 00:06:36.146
There were plenty of times in my century

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:39.566
when I was gonna give up,
but I never did. Never.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.360
Hey, are you even listening to me?

00:06:41.443 --> 00:06:42.736
Oh, I give up.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:44.988
By God, you're right!

00:06:45.072 --> 00:06:47.991
I'm going to build that Smelloscope.

00:06:50.244 --> 00:06:51.745
Eureka!

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:53.914
Did you build the Smelloscope?

00:06:53.997 --> 00:06:57.543
No. I remembered
that I built one last year.

00:06:58.335 --> 00:06:59.503
Go ahead, try it.

00:07:00.212 --> 00:07:04.424
You'll find that every heavenly body
has its own particular scent.

00:07:04.508 --> 00:07:06.844
Here, I'll point it at Jupiter.

00:07:11.306 --> 00:07:12.766
Smells like strawberries.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.101
Exactly.

00:07:14.184 --> 00:07:15.644
And now Saturn.

00:07:17.771 --> 00:07:19.022
Pine needles.

00:07:19.106 --> 00:07:20.440
Oh, man, this is great.

00:07:20.524 --> 00:07:22.818
As long as you don't make me smell Uranus.

00:07:22.901 --> 00:07:25.028
I don't get it.

00:07:25.112 --> 00:07:26.405
I'm sorry, Fry,

00:07:26.488 --> 00:07:29.700
but astronomers renamed Uranus in 2620

00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:32.286
to end that stupid joke once and for all.

00:07:32.369 --> 00:07:34.079
Oh. What's it called now?

00:07:34.162 --> 00:07:35.372
Urectum.

00:07:35.455 --> 00:07:38.166
- Here, let me locate it for you.
- How about...

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.837
No, no, I think I'll just
smell around a bit over here.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.964
Hmm.

00:07:44.631 --> 00:07:45.924
Hmm.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.223
What is it?

00:07:53.307 --> 00:07:55.100
Oh, jeez, oh, man!

00:07:55.184 --> 00:07:56.393
Remarkable!

00:07:56.476 --> 00:08:00.063
A stench so foul,
it's right off the funk-o-meter.

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:02.691
I daresay, Fry may have discovered

00:08:02.774 --> 00:08:05.611
the smelliest object
in the known universe.

00:08:05.694 --> 00:08:07.946
Ooh, ooh, name it after me!

00:08:09.323 --> 00:08:10.866
I think it's moving.

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:15.287
Hmm. Perhaps the computer
can calculate its trajectory.

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:16.705
My God!

00:08:16.788 --> 00:08:19.166
Whatever it is,
it's headed straight for us

00:08:19.249 --> 00:08:23.712
with enough force to reduce
this entire city to a stinky crater!

00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:26.256
We have less than 72 hours.

00:08:28.008 --> 00:08:29.510
Well, let's get lootin'.

00:08:35.891 --> 00:08:38.936
So this thing's gonna
destroy the whole city?

00:08:39.478 --> 00:08:40.521
What the heck is it?

00:08:42.105 --> 00:08:44.149
Ah, just as I thought.

00:08:44.233 --> 00:08:47.986
The answer lies in this movie
I found on the Internet.

00:08:50.906 --> 00:08:53.825
New York City, the year 2000.

00:08:53.909 --> 00:08:57.204
The most wasteful society
in the history of the galaxy,

00:08:57.287 --> 00:08:59.248
and it was running out of places

00:08:59.331 --> 00:09:02.709
to bury its never-ending
output of garbage.

00:09:02.793 --> 00:09:04.795
The landfills were full.

00:09:04.878 --> 00:09:06.839
New Jersey was full.

00:09:06.922 --> 00:09:09.049
And so, under cover of darkness,

00:09:09.132 --> 00:09:13.637
the city put its garbage out to sea
on the world's largest barge.

00:09:18.684 --> 00:09:22.271
The repulsive barge
circledthe oceans for 50 years

00:09:22.354 --> 00:09:24.064
but no country would accept it,

00:09:24.147 --> 00:09:26.859
not even that really filthy one.

00:09:26.942 --> 00:09:28.902
You know the one I mean.

00:09:28.986 --> 00:09:34.283
Finally, in 2052, the cityused
its mob connections to obtain a rocket

00:09:34.366 --> 00:09:37.578
and launch the garbage Into outer space.

00:09:37.661 --> 00:09:41.206
Some experts claim the ball
might return to Earth someday,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:45.043
but their concerns were dismissed
as "depressing."

00:09:45.127 --> 00:09:47.171
Wow, you got that off the Internet?

00:09:47.254 --> 00:09:50.549
In my day, the Internet was only used
to download pornography.

00:09:50.632 --> 00:09:52.968
Actually, that's still true.

00:09:53.051 --> 00:09:56.346
Now that the garbage is in space, doctor,

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.850
perhaps you can help me
with my sexual inhibitions.

00:09:59.933 --> 00:10:01.268
With gusto.

00:10:04.897 --> 00:10:06.148
Aw.

00:10:06.231 --> 00:10:07.941
So that's the situation.

00:10:08.025 --> 00:10:10.569
Due to the shortsightedness
of Old New York,

00:10:10.652 --> 00:10:15.199
New New York is going to be destroyed
by a giant ball of garbage.

00:10:15.282 --> 00:10:18.410
Fry, what the hell
were you people thinking back then?

00:10:18.493 --> 00:10:20.704
How could you just
throw your garbage away?

00:10:20.787 --> 00:10:22.247
Hey, hey, give me a break.

00:10:22.331 --> 00:10:23.665
What do you do with it?

00:10:23.749 --> 00:10:25.417
We recycle everything.

00:10:25.501 --> 00:10:27.878
Robots are made from old beer cans.

00:10:27.961 --> 00:10:30.964
Yeah, and this beer can
is made out of old robots.

00:10:31.048 --> 00:10:34.510
And that sandwich you're eating
is made from old discarded sandwiches.

00:10:34.593 --> 00:10:36.845
Nothing just gets thrown away.

00:10:36.929 --> 00:10:39.014
The future is disgusting.

00:10:39.097 --> 00:10:41.433
Typical 20th-century attitude.

00:10:41.517 --> 00:10:44.561
Hey, you have no right
to criticize the 20th century.

00:10:44.645 --> 00:10:48.315
We gave the world the light bulb,
the steamboat, and the cotton gin.

00:10:48.398 --> 00:10:50.984
Those things are all
from the 19th century.

00:10:51.068 --> 00:10:53.737
Yeah, well, they probably just copied us.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.198
Please, there's no time for this now.

00:10:56.281 --> 00:10:57.866
This is an emergency.

00:10:57.950 --> 00:10:59.660
We must warn the mayor.

00:11:00.577 --> 00:11:03.372
Garbage ball, huh? That sounds serious.

00:11:03.455 --> 00:11:05.874
Very serious, Mayor Poopenmeyer.

00:11:05.958 --> 00:11:08.377
I gotta sure this isn't
another scientific fraud

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.671
like global warming or secondhand smoke.

00:11:10.754 --> 00:11:13.090
Send in my science advisor.

00:11:14.675 --> 00:11:15.717
Wernstrom!

00:11:15.801 --> 00:11:16.927
Well, well, well.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.346
Come to present
your latest napkin, Professor?

00:11:19.429 --> 00:11:24.226
No, I'm here because a giant trash ball
is heading straight for us.

00:11:24.309 --> 00:11:25.894
Smell for yourself.

00:11:31.149 --> 00:11:32.860
Holy... Gee!

00:11:33.402 --> 00:11:35.529
That smell could be anything.

00:11:35.612 --> 00:11:38.991
A faulty stench coil,
some cheese on the lens, who knows?

00:11:39.074 --> 00:11:42.744
Mr. Mayor, we just got
this transmission from Neptune.

00:11:43.662 --> 00:11:47.749
Giant garbage ballpassed close by.

00:11:48.792 --> 00:11:51.420
Horrible stench!

00:11:55.215 --> 00:11:57.301
The transmission cuts out there, sir.

00:11:59.303 --> 00:12:02.139
No, I guess it keeps going.

00:12:06.518 --> 00:12:08.103
There we go.

00:12:08.187 --> 00:12:11.106
My God! The senile old man is right!

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:12.316
Do you mean him or me?

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:13.859
- Him.
- Oh.

00:12:17.321 --> 00:12:19.656
Next, New New York in crisis.

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.992
- Morbo?
- Thanks, human female.

00:12:22.075 --> 00:12:24.244
Puny Earthlings were shocked today

00:12:24.328 --> 00:12:26.538
to learn that a ball of garbage
will destroy

00:12:26.622 --> 00:12:28.582
their pathetic city of New New York.

00:12:28.665 --> 00:12:31.126
Makes me glad we live here in Los Angeles.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.711
Morbo agrees.

00:12:36.757 --> 00:12:38.759
Can't we just shoot a missile at it?

00:12:38.842 --> 00:12:41.303
We've simulated that on a supercomputer,

00:12:41.386 --> 00:12:43.472
but the ball is just too damn gooey.

00:12:43.555 --> 00:12:45.516
A missile would go right through it.

00:12:47.559 --> 00:12:50.854
But suppose we sent a crew
to plant an explosive

00:12:50.938 --> 00:12:52.564
precisely on the fault line

00:12:52.648 --> 00:12:54.650
between this mass of coffee grounds

00:12:54.733 --> 00:12:57.903
and this deposit
of America Online floppy disks.

00:13:00.197 --> 00:13:02.157
In theory, it could work.

00:13:02.241 --> 00:13:04.201
Uh, in theory, perhaps,

00:13:04.284 --> 00:13:08.121
but you'll never find a crew willing to
take on a mission so suicidally dangerous.

00:13:09.957 --> 00:13:11.333
Ah, crap.

00:13:11.917 --> 00:13:15.128
Now, you'll only have
one chance to destroy the ball.

00:13:15.212 --> 00:13:18.382
After that, it will be so close to Earth
that blowing it up

00:13:18.465 --> 00:13:21.844
would cause garbage to rain
over the entire planet,

00:13:21.927 --> 00:13:23.470
killing billions.

00:13:23.554 --> 00:13:24.847
Aw, boo-hoo.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.057
Now here's the bomb I've prepared.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:30.519
Once you activate it,
you'll have 25 minutes to get away.

00:13:30.602 --> 00:13:31.854
That's all? But...

00:13:31.879 --> 00:13:32.880
Now, now.

00:13:33.021 --> 00:13:35.399
There'll be plenty of time
to discuss your objections

00:13:35.482 --> 00:13:36.859
when and if you return.

00:13:49.663 --> 00:13:51.039
Odor at magnitude 8.

00:13:52.583 --> 00:13:54.877
Magnitude 12.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.588
Magnitude 31.

00:13:57.671 --> 00:14:00.632
We're breaking up.
Turn on the anti-smell device.

00:14:03.802 --> 00:14:05.387
Hmm, sporty.

00:14:10.267 --> 00:14:11.643
Wow!

00:14:11.727 --> 00:14:13.562
Look at all this filth.

00:14:13.645 --> 00:14:15.063
It's not filth.

00:14:15.147 --> 00:14:18.984
It's a glorious monument
to the achievements of the 20th century.

00:14:19.067 --> 00:14:20.903
Look, a real Beanie Baby.

00:14:21.028 --> 00:14:23.572
Oh, a Mr. Spock collectors' plate.

00:14:24.948 --> 00:14:26.575
Some Bart Simpson dolls.

00:14:29.369 --> 00:14:30.495
Eat my shorts.

00:14:30.579 --> 00:14:31.747
Okay.

00:14:32.456 --> 00:14:34.541
Mmm, shorts.

00:14:34.625 --> 00:14:37.169
Fry, this stuff
was garbage when it was new.

00:14:37.252 --> 00:14:38.670
Let's blow it up already.

00:14:38.754 --> 00:14:40.339
This junk isn't garbage.

00:14:40.422 --> 00:14:43.592
I can dig in any random pile
and find something great.

00:14:53.519 --> 00:14:55.771
All right, let's get to work.

00:15:01.318 --> 00:15:02.861
Let's see.

00:15:02.945 --> 00:15:07.032
If that's hypodermic ridge,
then the bomb must go right here.

00:15:11.119 --> 00:15:12.120
Get ready to run.

00:15:12.204 --> 00:15:13.413
We've got 25 minutes.

00:15:14.331 --> 00:15:15.791
Uh, 15 minutes.

00:15:16.875 --> 00:15:18.168
Five minutes.

00:15:18.252 --> 00:15:19.545
Six-h minutes?

00:15:20.629 --> 00:15:21.797
Hmm.

00:15:23.257 --> 00:15:24.758
There's your problem.

00:15:24.842 --> 00:15:27.970
The professor put
the counter on upside down.

00:15:28.053 --> 00:15:29.137
That idiot.

00:15:29.221 --> 00:15:30.973
It wasn't set for 25 minutes.

00:15:31.056 --> 00:15:32.975
It was set for 52 seconds.

00:15:33.058 --> 00:15:35.561
Ahhh! We're gonna die!

00:15:35.644 --> 00:15:36.895
- Right?
- Right.

00:15:36.979 --> 00:15:38.146
Ahhh!

00:15:43.944 --> 00:15:46.238
It's gonna blow!

00:15:49.241 --> 00:15:51.785
Hey, watch it!
You'll put somebody's eye out!

00:15:51.869 --> 00:15:54.246
Okay, okay, keep your space pants on.

00:15:54.329 --> 00:15:55.706
I'll take care of this.

00:16:04.298 --> 00:16:05.507
We're saved.

00:16:05.591 --> 00:16:08.343
Yeah, but this garbage ball's
unstoppable now.

00:16:08.427 --> 00:16:10.220
New New York is done for.

00:16:15.851 --> 00:16:20.105
All in all, this is one day
Mittens the kitten won't soon forget.

00:16:22.649 --> 00:16:24.902
Kittens give Morbo gas.

00:16:24.985 --> 00:16:28.488
In lighter news,
the city of New New York is doomed.

00:16:28.572 --> 00:16:32.075
Blame rests with known human
Professor Hubert Farnsworth

00:16:32.159 --> 00:16:34.745
and his tiny inferior brain.

00:16:35.245 --> 00:16:39.875
Aw! How could I have put
that bomb timer on upside down?

00:16:39.958 --> 00:16:43.253
I could swear I followed
the manual precisely.

00:16:43.337 --> 00:16:46.089
I'm a dried-up husk of a scientist.

00:16:46.173 --> 00:16:48.133
This is all my fault.

00:16:48.217 --> 00:16:50.385
No, it's my fault, too.

00:16:50.469 --> 00:16:53.972
I'm sure I threw out
more than my share of that trash up there.

00:16:54.115 --> 00:16:56.107
Also, one month, my toilet broke

00:16:56.132 --> 00:16:58.051
and I just went straight
in the garbage can.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:00.771
Leela was right.

00:17:00.854 --> 00:17:04.233
The people of the 20th century
were idiotic slobs.

00:17:04.316 --> 00:17:05.567
Especially me.

00:17:05.651 --> 00:17:07.736
Enough. You all failed miserably.

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:09.738
It's time to put
a real scientist in charge.

00:17:11.573 --> 00:17:13.825
- Wernstrom!
- The very same.

00:17:13.909 --> 00:17:16.245
Dr. Wernstrom, can you save my city?

00:17:16.328 --> 00:17:18.372
Of course, but it'll cost you.

00:17:18.455 --> 00:17:20.415
- First, I'll need tenure.
- Done.

00:17:20.499 --> 00:17:22.626
- And a big research grant.
- You got it.

00:17:22.709 --> 00:17:25.295
Also access to a lab
and five graduate students,

00:17:25.379 --> 00:17:26.964
at least three of them Chinese.

00:17:27.047 --> 00:17:29.591
All right. All right, done.

00:17:29.675 --> 00:17:31.510
- What's your plan?
- What plan?

00:17:31.593 --> 00:17:33.136
I'm set for life.

00:17:33.679 --> 00:17:35.138
Au revoir, suckers.

00:17:35.764 --> 00:17:37.933
That rat. Do something.

00:17:38.016 --> 00:17:40.227
I wish I could, but he's got tenure.

00:17:52.656 --> 00:17:54.491
Hmm?

00:17:56.535 --> 00:17:58.787
It's time to take action.

00:17:58.871 --> 00:18:02.207
Stephanie, cancel the maid for today.
Have her come tomorrow.

00:18:02.291 --> 00:18:04.251
Well, I'm out of ideas.

00:18:04.334 --> 00:18:05.335
Anyone?

00:18:05.419 --> 00:18:06.503
Wait.

00:18:06.587 --> 00:18:08.255
If we could build an object

00:18:08.338 --> 00:18:12.217
the exact size, density,
and consistency of the garbage ball,

00:18:12.301 --> 00:18:15.762
it might just knock the ball away
without smashing it to bits.

00:18:16.638 --> 00:18:18.307
But where can we find a substance

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:21.351
the exact density
and consistency as garbage?

00:18:21.435 --> 00:18:23.604
Alas, I don't know.

00:18:23.687 --> 00:18:25.606
Uh, what about garbage?

00:18:25.689 --> 00:18:26.940
Good Lord!

00:18:27.024 --> 00:18:30.444
A second ball of garbage.
That just might work.

00:18:30.527 --> 00:18:34.031
But garbage isn't something you just find
lying in the streets of Manhattan.

00:18:34.114 --> 00:18:36.575
This city's been garbage-free
for 500 years.

00:18:36.658 --> 00:18:38.535
Well, then it's time to make some more.

00:18:38.619 --> 00:18:41.205
Make garbage? But how?

00:18:41.288 --> 00:18:44.166
Stand back and watch the master.

00:18:44.249 --> 00:18:45.751
This Slurm can?

00:18:46.543 --> 00:18:47.753
Now it's garbage.

00:18:47.836 --> 00:18:48.962
These papers?

00:18:49.588 --> 00:18:51.548
Garbage.
This picture of your wife?

00:18:52.633 --> 00:18:55.260
Pure garbage. Now you try it.

00:19:00.474 --> 00:19:03.018
By God, I think the boy's got something.

00:19:03.101 --> 00:19:05.687
Come on, everyone.
The fate of the city's at stake.

00:19:06.355 --> 00:19:07.356
Good.

00:19:07.439 --> 00:19:09.566
Don't finish that cruller. Throw it away.

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:12.569
Bender, drink that beer
and drop the bottle on the ground.

00:19:12.803 --> 00:19:13.970
Very nice.

00:19:14.054 --> 00:19:15.722
Get that robot some more beer.

00:19:15.806 --> 00:19:16.848
Hmm?

00:19:16.932 --> 00:19:19.059
We've trashed this room
but that's just a start.

00:19:19.142 --> 00:19:21.687
We've got to get Fry's message
to the people.

00:19:22.604 --> 00:19:26.858
People of New New York,
take a lesson from the 20th century,

00:19:26.942 --> 00:19:31.738
stop all this pain-in-the-ass recycling
and throw your garbage on the floor.

00:19:31.822 --> 00:19:34.950
Go ahead, just chuck it
any old place like I used to.

00:19:35.033 --> 00:19:36.785
Your city is counting on you.

00:19:49.006 --> 00:19:50.865
If my calculations are correct,

00:19:50.919 --> 00:19:53.672
this garbage ball will knock
the other garbage ball

00:19:53.735 --> 00:19:55.404
directly into the sun.

00:19:55.487 --> 00:19:57.447
And if my calculations are correct,

00:19:57.531 --> 00:19:59.658
we're all going to die horribly.

00:19:59.741 --> 00:20:01.410
Ha-ha-ha-ha, ha-ha!

00:20:01.493 --> 00:20:02.494
Ha...

00:20:02.578 --> 00:20:04.162
All right, places, everyone.

00:20:04.246 --> 00:20:05.873
Prepare for launch.

00:20:05.956 --> 00:20:10.294
Five, four, three, two...

00:20:10.836 --> 00:20:14.423
Three, four, five, six...

00:20:14.506 --> 00:20:16.550
Just fire the damn thing.

00:20:18.385 --> 00:20:19.428
Oops.

00:20:51.168 --> 00:20:53.921
Burning garbage.

00:20:54.004 --> 00:20:55.130
It worked!

00:20:58.175 --> 00:21:00.969
And so, on behalf of the entire city,

00:21:01.053 --> 00:21:03.222
I thank you, Professor Farnsworth.

00:21:03.305 --> 00:21:07.601
I now present you with the Academy prize
which we confiscated from Dr. Wernstrom

00:21:07.684 --> 00:21:10.145
after it became apparent
that he was a jackass.

00:21:11.855 --> 00:21:14.775
Yes! In your face, Wernstrom!

00:21:14.858 --> 00:21:18.320
I'll get you, Farnsworth,
even if it takes another hundred years.

00:21:19.821 --> 00:21:23.075
And, Fry, we owe you
a tremendous debt as well.

00:21:23.158 --> 00:21:26.119
Were it not for
your 20th-century garbage-making skills,

00:21:26.203 --> 00:21:29.373
we'd all be buried
under 20th-century garbage.

00:21:31.834 --> 00:21:33.460
Should we really be celebrating?

00:21:33.544 --> 00:21:37.548
I mean, what if this second garbage ball
returns to Earth like the first one did?

00:21:37.631 --> 00:21:40.759
Well, who cares?
That won't be for hundreds of years.

00:21:40.843 --> 00:21:43.971
Exactly. It's none of our concern.

00:21:44.054 --> 00:21:46.598
That's the 20th-century spirit.

00:21:54.106 --> 00:21:59.194
We'll meet again

00:21:59.778 --> 00:22:02.281
Don't know where

00:22:02.364 --> 00:22:05.325
And I don't know when

00:22:05.409 --> 00:22:10.873
But I know we'll meet again

00:22:10.956 --> 00:22:15.335
Some sunny day

00:22:17.421 --> 00:22:23.969
Oh, keep smiling through